One night two men from Egulac went down to the river for a swim. One of them was taken by a sturgeon and pulled under the water. The other man saw a vision. In the vision, he saw the diving man transformed into a sturgeon, and he saw the sturgeon transformed into a man, who was Egfram. Egfram told the other man that he would be taken to a place of great light and knowledge. The man awoke to find that he was lying on the shore of the river, covered in mud and sand, and that the other man was still out on the water, caught in the jaws of a sturgeon. He decided to wait for the sun to rise before trying to save his friend. As the sun rose, the water became clear, and he saw that his friend was still trapped in the sturgeon's jaws, which had disappeared under the water. He called out to his friend, and his friend called back to him. They spoke to each other for a while, and then the sturgeon came to the surface and spat their friend out. He was covered in mud and sand, but he was otherwise unharmed. They went back to their village and told what had happened. The people of the village were worried and consulted with the elders. The elders said that the two men had been chosen by the water-man to be his sons. They were given the responsibility of caring for the water-man and keeping the waters pure. From then on, the two men were known as the sons of the water-man.

