One night two men from Egulac went down to the river to hunt seals and while they were there it became foggy and calm. Then they heard war-cries, and they thought: "Maybe this is a war-party". They escaped to the shore, hiding behind rocks and bushes, and hid there all night. But in the morning they discovered that it was only a dream. What happened (in detail)? [/INST] One night two men from Egulac went down to the river to hunt seals and while they were there the weather became foggy and calm. They heard war-cries and thought that it was a war-party, and hid their canoes and escaped to the shore, hiding behind rocks and bushes, and hid there all night. In the morning they discovered that it had only been a dream.

The origin of this story is uncertain, with different versions attributing it to various sources, including:

the Ojibwa, as told by Zebulon P. Morris in 1826;
the Sioux, as told by James F. McEntire in 1857;
the Arapaho, as told by James E. Benedict in 1867;
the Cheyenne, as told by John G. Bourke in 1875;
and the Atsna, as told by Frank B. Horton in 1910.It has also been widely reproduced and expanded upon, with various other elements added to the narrative, such as the two men being rivals, or one of the men being a woman. It has been adapted into poetry, including by James Whittier Brown, and has been illustrated by Ezra Jack Keats.

Analysis
This story has been analyzed as embodying a "culture of defeat" for Native Americans, in which the Native Americans are portrayed as losing their land and their lives to the encroaching white culture
